#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd566f7-15e2-59d9-bf6a-1fa3598399b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Composite Function Using a Standard Maclaurin Expansion"}] :question/id #uuid "895adb0f-347a-52e1-a160-3fbba31a4c49" :question/math-academy-id "e-754" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\sin{(2x^2)}$." :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\sin{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x &= x - \\frac{x^{3}}{3!} + \\frac{x^{5}}{5!} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x^2$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (2x^{2}) &= (2x^{2}) - ((2x^{2})^{3})/(3!) + ((2x^{2})^{5})/(5!) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} - \\frac{8x^{6}}{6} + \\frac{32x^{10}}{120} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} - \\frac{4x^{6}}{3} + \\frac{4x^{10}}{15} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series has an infinite radius of convergence because the series expansion for $\\sin x$ has an infinite radius of convergence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffddeaa7-a247-54b0-af4b-a29d9bbeee6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/id #uuid "7f8c3610-ab9a-5bee-bcc5-a817a79b50cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-755" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $e^x+\\sin(2x)$." :question/worked-solution "The Maclaurin series for $e^x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x} &= 1 + x + \\frac{1}{2!}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3!}x^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6}x^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin series for $\\sin{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sin x = x - \\frac{1}{3!}x^{3} + ⋯\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (2x) &= 2x - \\frac{1}{3!}(2x)^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 2x - \\frac{4}{3}x^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the expansions for $e^{x}$ and $\\sin(2x)$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x} + \\sin (2x) &= (1 + x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6}x^{3} + ⋯) + (2x - \\frac{4}{3}x^{3} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= 1 + (1 + 2)x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + (\\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{4}{3})x^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 3x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} - \\frac{7}{6}x^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series has an infinite radius of convergence because the series expansions for $e^x$ and $\\sin x$ both have an infinite radius of convergence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b839f19a-161c-5cb4-bc08-990f09afb900" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e29f6eb6-9b2a-5f03-aad3-3bc36dbe400e" :question/math-academy-id "e-756" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the expansion of $f(x) = \\ln{\\left(\\dfrac{1+2x}{1+x}\\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (\\frac{1 + 2x}{1 + x}) &= \\ln (1 + 2x) - \\ln (1 + x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the expansion of $\\ln(1+x)$, we use the standard result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 + x) &= x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} - ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the expansion of $\\ln{(1+2x)}$, we replace $x$ with $2x$ in the standard expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 + 2x) &= 2x - \\frac{1}{2}(2x)^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}(2x)^{3} - ⋯ \\\\\n&= 2x - 2x^{2} + \\frac{8}{3}x^{3} - ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the two series to give the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (\\frac{1 + 2x}{1 + x}) &= \\ln (1 + 2x) - \\ln (1 + x) \\\\\n&= (2x - 2x^{2} + \\frac{8}{3}x^{3} - ⋯) - (x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} - ⋯) \\\\\n&= x - \\frac{3}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{7}{3}x^{3} - ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the series $\\ln(1+2x)$, the radius of convergence is given by\n\n$[MATH: -1 < 2x \\leq 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right].]$\n\nThe series $\\ln(1+x)$ has a radius of convergence of $[MATH: x\\in(-1,1].]$ Therefore, the radius of convergence of $f(x)$ is\n\n$[MATH: x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right] \\cap (-1,1] = \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right].]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de71fcb6-36ca-581a-92ea-47c1d0c6bf10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Product of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/id #uuid "f4e495eb-3712-5106-abce-ab07f15fb328" :question/math-academy-id "e-757" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $e^x \\cos{x}$." :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $e^x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x} &= 1 + x + \\frac{1}{2!}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3!}x^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6}x^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\cos{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x &= 1 - \\frac{1}{2!}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{4!}x^{4} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{24}x^{4} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of $e^x \\cos{x}$, we multiply the expansion of $e^x$ and $\\cos{x}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x}\\cos x &= (1 + x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6}x^{3} + ⋯)(1 - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{24}x^{4} + ⋯)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n- There is a single constant term, given by\n$1 \\cdot 1 = 1$.\n- There is a single $x$ term, given by\n$x \\cdot 1 = x$.\n- There are two $x^2$ terms which cancel out\n$1 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}) + (\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}) \\cdot 1|=-\\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}; = 0$.\n- There are two $x^3$ terms and their sum is given by\n$x \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}) + (\\frac{1}{6}x^{3}) \\cdot 1|=-\\frac{1}{2}x^{3} + \\frac{1}{6}x^{3}; =-\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}$.\n\nSo, the first three non-zero terms of the expansion are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x}\\cos x &= 1 + x - \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series has an infinite radius of convergence because the series expansions for $e^x$ and $\\cos x$ both have an infinite radius of convergence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffddeaa7-a247-54b0-af4b-a29d9bbeee6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a15aac4f-4314-564c-b5db-ac03b745913b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x^{2}} - e^{-x^{2}} = 2x^{2} + \\frac{x^{6}}{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084646} {:answer/id #uuid "e825eba9-0915-512c-9410-6ff70a105e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x^{2}} - e^{-x^{2}} = 2x^{2} + \\frac{x^{4}}{6} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084647} {:answer/id #uuid "2a61eb25-9d09-5488-8523-aa584340edac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x^{2}} - e^{-x^{2}} = x + \\frac{x^{3}}{6} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084648} {:answer/id #uuid "2fee8eef-ab46-54e2-93c0-8e85fa8f75f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x^{2}} - e^{-x^{2}} = \\frac{x^{2}}{2} - \\frac{x^{6}}{24} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084649} {:answer/id #uuid "49d50da8-dca8-51ca-acd8-7d249807fca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x^{2}} - e^{-x^{2}} = \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{x^{3}}{24} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a15aac4f-4314-564c-b5db-ac03b745913b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x^{2}} - e^{-x^{2}} = 2x^{2} + \\frac{x^{6}}{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084646} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c9372fb-315f-51fc-aa08-381ec1e8f706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084645}] :question/id #uuid "4ae842d7-35ba-5ff7-97ff-de4c3d47ebaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-12437" :question/problem "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $e^{x^{2}} - e^{-x^{2}}$."}]}
